<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Copyright (C) 2026 SkapaCraft <https://skapacraft.com>
  SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later

  Mac App Store entitlements. This is the only build of Nostos on any platform
  that requests a network permission, and the reason is worth stating plainly
  rather than burying.

  App Sandbox is mandatory on the Mac App Store. Under it, WKWebView will not
  start: its WebContent process is refused at launch, exits with status 1, and
  the application shows an empty window while the webview restarts forever.
  Measured on this project, signed both ways from the same binary: without
  network.client, 1836 WebContent restarts in nine seconds; with it, none. The
  process is denied reads of /Library/Preferences/com.apple.networkd.plist and
  /private/var/db/nsurlstoraged/dafsaData.bin, which WebKit needs to bring up
  its networking process even for a page that will only ever load from disk.

  So this entitlement is what the operating system charges to render local HTML
  in a sandbox, not a capability the application wanted. What it does not
  change:

  - No networking library exists in the dependency graph. cargo-deny fails the
    build if one appears, and that check runs on every change.
  - The content security policy still limits connect-src to the local IPC
    channel, so a fetch added by mistake is refused by the webview.
  - No code in this application opens a connection, because there is nothing
    in it that can.

  What it does change, and the reason the wording was revised everywhere it
  appeared: on this build the sandbox no longer makes a connection impossible
  at the OS level. It remains absent from the code. Every other build of
  Nostos, the Flatpak included, still has no network permission at all.

  application-identifier and team-identifier must match the provisioning
  profile exactly or the upload is rejected. They are absent from the
  non-store entitlements because a Developer ID build carrying them fails
  notarisation instead.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.application-identifier</key>
    <string>8Z8ZP4SX98.com.skapacraft.nostos</string>
    <key>com.apple.developer.team-identifier</key>
    <string>8Z8ZP4SX98</string>
    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
